WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.060 --> 00:00:03.000
As author Jenny Han has said, 'P.S.

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:08.490
I Still Love You is about the reality of really
loving somebody and not just falling in love

00:00:08.490 --> 00:00:09.490
with somebody.

00:00:09.490 --> 00:00:12.980
This is the part of the story that you don't
often get to see in movies, which is what

00:00:12.980 --> 00:00:15.200
happens after the couple gets together.'

00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:20.099
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers, I'm Jan and in
this video I'm breaking down how LJ made her

00:00:20.099 --> 00:00:21.810
crucial decision at the end of P.S.

00:00:21.810 --> 00:00:22.810
I Still Love You.

00:00:22.810 --> 00:00:27.079
There will, of course, be spoilers, so take
care if you haven't seen the movie yet.

00:00:27.079 --> 00:00:32.290
LJ may be deliriously happy at the prospect
of their first date, but reality doesn't live

00:00:32.290 --> 00:00:37.110
up to the fantasy of her romance novels, and
things don't turn out quite as perfectly as

00:00:37.110 --> 00:00:38.180
she imagined.

00:00:38.180 --> 00:00:42.440
Lara Jean finds Peter's goofing around at
the restaurant a little embarrassing, and

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:46.900
the moment feels less special when she discovers
he regularly took his ex, Genevieve, to the

00:00:46.900 --> 00:00:47.900
same restaurant.

00:00:47.900 --> 00:00:51.800
"For every first I was having with him, he'd
already had his with her."

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:56.540
LJ also feels let down by Peter when she finds
out that the Valentine's Day poem she thought

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:00.690
he wrote specially for her was actually written
by Edgar Allan Poe.

00:01:00.690 --> 00:01:05.150
And then there's the way Peter turns up hours
late for a coffee date with LJ as he prioritised

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:07.250
sports practice over her.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:12.270
But the issues they're having with their relationship
aren't entirely on Peter, because when Lara

00:01:12.270 --> 00:01:17.280
Jean unexpectedly receives a letter from childhood
crush John Ambrose, emotions and feelings

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:20.469
she didn't expect to have any longer rush
back to the surface.

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:25.100
"I thought having a boyfriend meant the mere
idea of other boys left your mind completely."

00:01:25.100 --> 00:01:30.110
As Lana Condor, who plays LJ, told EW,
"[John] is kind of like the male version of

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:31.340
Lara Jean.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:33.170
Everything she loves, he loves."

00:01:33.170 --> 00:01:37.420
For example, they're both House Hufflepuff,
which LJ once thought could be a sign they

00:01:37.420 --> 00:01:39.170
were destined for each other.

00:01:39.170 --> 00:01:43.570
Lara Jean doesn't know how to handle these
old feelings and memories, and tries to suppress

00:01:43.570 --> 00:01:46.560
them by putting away the letter she's writing
to John Ambrose.

00:01:46.560 --> 00:01:48.109
"Oh my God.

00:01:48.109 --> 00:01:49.170
I cannot do this.

00:01:49.170 --> 00:01:51.149
Do I have to tell Peter about this?"

00:01:51.149 --> 00:01:55.249
But when she meets him again and reads her
original childhood letter which mentions their

00:01:55.249 --> 00:02:00.630
shared embarrassment during a Halloween party,
she remembers how close she felt to him.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:03.299
"I love you John Ambrose, I really love you.

00:02:03.299 --> 00:02:06.869
I'll always know that once upon a time, my
heart was yours."

00:02:06.869 --> 00:02:11.629
As she explores these emotions, Lara Jean
realises she feels much more comfortable around

00:02:11.629 --> 00:02:15.760
John compared to the constant worrying she
feels and overthinking she does about her

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:16.991
relationship with Peter.

00:02:16.991 --> 00:02:20.120
"But if Peter was sitting here and we were
sharing an ice cream sandwich, I dunno..

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.270
I'd be thinking about has Peter shared an
ice cream sandwich with someone before?

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:28.450
Does he think that I'm a prude if I just wanna
share an ice cream sandwich?"

00:02:28.450 --> 00:02:32.160
"I just need a break from the contortions
of being someone's girlfriend."

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:36.659
The developing love triangle creates problems
for Peter and LJ's relationship when he gets

00:02:36.659 --> 00:02:40.090
jealous that she's spending time with John
Ambrose at Belleview.

00:02:40.090 --> 00:02:44.000
It's no coincidence that this comes up in
the middle of a biology class during which

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:47.019
the teacher talks about octopuses having three
hearts.

00:02:47.019 --> 00:02:51.129
And there's a clever bit of foreshadowing
of their break-up when Lara Jean dissects

00:02:51.129 --> 00:02:54.620
the octopus's heart in front of a squeamish
Peter.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:58.620
Things come to a head at their old treehouse
haunt where Peter gets possessive and John

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:03.090
finds out that LJ and Peter are together,
something she hadn't told him.

00:03:03.090 --> 00:03:08.019
Peter complains LJ shouldn't be hanging around
with her ex-crush, an accusation that seems

00:03:08.019 --> 00:03:11.569
a bit rich given the amount of time he spends
with Gen.

00:03:11.569 --> 00:03:15.739
Although they patch things up, they argue
again later about Peter's close relationship

00:03:15.739 --> 00:03:20.620
with Gen, and LJ has the heart-breaking realisation
that on the night of the hot tub, Peter was

00:03:20.620 --> 00:03:23.150
actually waiting for Gen, not her.

00:03:23.150 --> 00:03:27.760
LJ's mistrust of Peter is too deep and they
break up at the aquarium, which was hinted

00:03:27.760 --> 00:03:30.439
at earlier during the octopus heart scene.

00:03:30.439 --> 00:03:34.690
Ultimately, the root of their problem is the
secrets they've been keeping from each other.

00:03:34.690 --> 00:03:39.900
Their intentions weren't necessarily bad,
as each was trying to protect the other.

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:43.939
Peter keeps the time he spends with Gen trying
to help with her parents' problem hidden from

00:03:43.939 --> 00:03:47.069
Lara Jean because he knows there's bad blood
between them.

00:03:47.069 --> 00:03:51.379
And likewise, LJ keeps her feelings for John
Ambrose to herself out of fear of hurting

00:03:51.379 --> 00:03:52.379
Peter.

00:03:52.379 --> 00:03:55.890
"I thought that if Peter and I were together,
the two of us could get through anything.

00:03:55.890 --> 00:03:56.890
I was wrong.

00:03:56.890 --> 00:03:59.370
But I do know we weren't honest with each
other.

00:03:59.370 --> 00:04:01.549
And that I haven't been honest with myself."

00:04:01.549 --> 00:04:06.269
The fallout from their break-up leads Lara
Jean to briefly kiss John Ambrose at the Belleview

00:04:06.269 --> 00:04:11.030
ball, in a moment which feels straight out
of one of the romance novels LJ loves so much.

00:04:11.030 --> 00:04:15.859
But this is reality, not fantasy, and she
quickly realises she doesn't like him that

00:04:15.859 --> 00:04:16.859
way.

00:04:16.859 --> 00:04:17.859
"We kissed.

00:04:17.859 --> 00:04:21.530
But I wanted him to be someone else."

00:04:21.530 --> 00:04:25.310
And Stormy helps her understand that:
"Sometimes you have to kiss the wrong man

00:04:25.310 --> 00:04:27.680
to know what's right."

00:04:27.680 --> 00:04:32.550
Although LJ and John are [theoretically] more
compatible in terms of their shared interests,

00:04:32.550 --> 00:04:37.430
outlook and temperament, it's Peter who offers
Lara Jean something more and gives her scope

00:04:37.430 --> 00:04:39.800
for personal development and growth.

00:04:39.800 --> 00:04:44.470
As Lana Condor says, "Lara Jean […] needs
to be pushed to expand into different spaces

00:04:44.470 --> 00:04:48.550
of her personality, and that's what Peter
does, even though he presents the risk of

00:04:48.550 --> 00:04:49.680
breaking her heart."

00:04:49.680 --> 00:04:54.139
In fact, according to the sequel's director,
Lara Jean and Peter's promise to never hurt

00:04:54.139 --> 00:04:59.240
each other was something that was impossible
to keep, a youthful but naïve vow, and when

00:04:59.240 --> 00:05:02.240
they go through that in this movie, they actually
emerge stronger.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:04.940
"Peter and I promised not to break each other's
hearts."

00:05:04.940 --> 00:05:07.900
"Oh, honey, you're always breaking someone's
heart, or they're breaking yours."

00:05:07.900 --> 00:05:10.850
"Well, isn't there a way to both get out unscathed?"

00:05:10.850 --> 00:05:12.949
"Not if you're doing it right."

00:05:12.949 --> 00:05:15.319
"Break my heart into a thousand pieces.

00:05:15.319 --> 00:05:17.169
Do whatever you want."

00:05:17.169 --> 00:05:20.930
Also at the heart of the sequel is a resolution
to the question of whether it's OK to have

00:05:20.930 --> 00:05:22.790
feelings for more than one person.

00:05:22.790 --> 00:05:28.000
After her break-up with Peter, Lara Jean realises
there's a concept from her Korean heritage

00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:30.860
that can help her with this:
"It's called jung.

00:05:30.860 --> 00:05:33.759
It's the connection between two people that
can't be severed.

00:05:33.759 --> 00:05:37.509
Even when love turns to hate, you will always
have tenderness in your heart for them."

00:05:37.509 --> 00:05:42.220
LJ notices that even though they're no longer
friends, she and Gen still have jung.

00:05:42.220 --> 00:05:46.940
And she realises that Peter also has it with
Gen and that she should stop blaming him for

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:47.940
it.

00:05:47.940 --> 00:05:52.070
This moment of self-discovery helps her reconcile
with Genevieve, and in the next scene when

00:05:52.070 --> 00:05:56.889
Lara Jean sees her father and Mrs Rothschild
together, she finally realises it's possible

00:05:56.889 --> 00:06:01.220
for him to find love with someone else, but
still hold affection for their mother.

00:06:01.220 --> 00:06:06.009
Likewise, by the end of the movie, LJ recognises
that it's possible to love two people as the

00:06:06.009 --> 00:06:08.800
same time, albeit in different ways.

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:14.020
As Lana Condor said in an interview with EW,
LJ's deep love for John can be confusing "because

00:06:14.020 --> 00:06:17.740
you're like, 'Oh man, do I love him or do
I love him as a friend?'"

00:06:17.740 --> 00:06:22.030
The concept of jung though means that Lara
Jean doesn't have to forsake her deep affection

00:06:22.030 --> 00:06:24.870
for John in order to remain faithful to Peter.

00:06:24.870 --> 00:06:27.430
"Are we good John Ambrose?

00:06:27.430 --> 00:06:29.490
We've always been good."

00:06:29.490 --> 00:06:34.160
Or as the movie's director puts it, "much
of what the sequel is about is Lara Jean learning

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:38.410
to confront her own truth without losing her
optimistic outlook on love."

00:06:38.410 --> 00:06:41.240
"It's not a cynical view of romance or adolescence.

00:06:41.240 --> 00:06:44.980
It's an earnest, fun, passionate presentation
of it."

00:06:44.980 --> 00:06:49.280
Now, what do you think about the sequel and
how it handled the love triangle?

00:06:49.280 --> 00:06:54.009
And are you glad LJ ended up back with Peter
or would you have preferred John Ambrose?

